{{also|tabulá|tabulā}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|tabula}}. {{doublet|en|table}}. {{lena}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|tabulae|pl2qual=archaic|tabulæ}}

# A [[plate]] or [[frame]] on which a [[title]] or [[inscription]] is [[carve]]d.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937| title=Memoirs - Issues 4-6 |author=Kyancutta Museum | page=103| passage=The '''tabula''' is here seen to have an irregular structure of coarse mesh whose interstices are occupied by a finer mesh. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1982 | title=Supplementum Epigraphicum Graecum [SEG]: 1978 |author=H. W. Pleket, ‎R. S. Stroud | page=227| passage=A.D. Fragment of an inscription in a''' tabula''' carved in the rock. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1989| title=Aphrodisias in Late Antiquity|author=‎Charlotte Roueché, ‎Joyce Maire Reynolds | page=| passage=The date is written above the '''tabula''' and down the right side, which is worn; the main text is written within the '''tabula''', with each line of verse separated by an incised line. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997 | title=Arai Epitymbioi: Imprecations Against Desecrators of the Grave in the Greek Epitaphs of Asis Minor|author=J. H. M. Strubbe | page=210| passage=The front side has a raised border; it is decorated with a '''tabula''' between two shields.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005| title=Menderes Magnesiası|author=Orhan Bingöl | page=42| passage=The lower profile is finished from the first niche (N1) up to the second '''tabula''' (T2), but to the west of T2, at O10, only its astragal is complete.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006| title=Roman Portrait Statuary from Aphrodisias |author=R. R. R. Smith | page=245| passage=It was worked separately from its foot but with its '''tabula''' which is decorated with a square, four-petaled rosette in relief.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009| title=Dirāsāt Fī Tārīkh Wa-āthār Al-Urdun | volume = 10 |author=| page=762| passage=... part of an ecclesiastical complex which included, to the south-east, a larger church which we called the Church of the Tabula Ansata after an inscription framed in a '''tabula''' incised on a gypsum slab in the paved floor of the presbyterium.}}
# A [[table]], [[index]], or [[list]] of [[data]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1908 | title=Early Compotus Rolls of the Priory of Worcester |author=James Maurice Wilson, ‎Cosmo Alexander Gordon | page=28| passage=Another '''tabula''' on third fly-leaf, and some writing in a later hand. Titles and initials throughout in red and blue.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1962| title=Neoplasma|author=| page=221| passage=Another '''tabula''' represents the rate of morbidity according to different organs and age groups. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992| title=Venues for Contexture: A Critical Analysis and Enactive Reformulation of Group Decision Support Systems|author=Randall Whitaker | page=157| passage=This may be done on the spot, or the initial '''tabula(e)''' may be delineated prior to the meeting (e.g., via an agreed agenda or results from a prior session). The ongoing course of tabulation will then be an open-ended process alternating between propagation of '''tabulae''' and discursive refinement of the emerging tabular structure.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008| title=Increased Exchange in the Building Sector|author=| page=72| passage=The '''tabula''' displays that interpretation of planning content has more focus in the Nordic group of countries (except Iceland), than in the Baltic group – even if the countries have chosen different tools. }}
# A [[legal]] [[record]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1850| title=A Pilgrimage to the Land of My Fathers - Volume 1 |author=Moses Margoliouth | page=144| passage=However, as Ugolinus is known as an industrious, honourable man, acquainted with his subject, and who cannot easily be suspected of fraud, there is nothing against assuming the probability that at the publication of his work he had really before him such a '''tabula'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1912 | title=The Distinctions and Anomalies Arising Out of the Equitable Doctrine of the Legal Estate|author=Roland Moffatt Perowne Willoughby | page=74| passage=On the other hand, the acquisition of the legal estate merely as a '''tabula''' is usually not a transaction for value, and here at any rate the fact that it is conveyed in breach of an express trust will prevail to take it away.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013| title=Maternal Megalomania: Julia Domna and the Imperial Politics of Motherhood|author=Julie Langford | page=23| passage=The soldiers who dedicated the '''tabula''' to the empress might have had a personal connection with her, but if so, it was not a typical relationship. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=Legalism: Community and Justice|author=Fernanda Pirie, ‎Judith Scheele | page=| passage=However, proclamation was to be made of the offence and the misdeed inscribed on a ''''tabula'''' in the Guildhall, so that citizens and other inhabitants might be warned not to employ him in any spiritual office.}}
# A [[writing]]-[[tablet]], [[slate]], or similar medium on which to write.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010| title=A Lily of the Field|author=John Lawton | page=| passage=On the '''tabula''' of the mind she tried to write the word.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=A Breath of Frost |author=Alyxandra Harvey | page=403| passage=Can we send them a message? With your '''tabula''' perhaps, the way Olwen did when Moira needed you?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=The Testimonies of Russian and American Postmodern Poetry|author=Albena Lutzkanova-Vassileva | page=60| passage=The mind is a '''tabula''' to engrave the story of reality's cataclysmic impacts, the individual—a tool through which history confides it to mankind.}}
# A [[frontal]]; a [[drapery]] for an [[altar]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004 | title=Textilien des Mittelmeerraumes aus spätantiker bis tru:hisiamischer Zeit |author=Sabine Schrenk, ‎Abegg-Stiftung | page=470| passage=Another '''tabula''' in the Textile Museum in Washington D.C. probably belonged to the same textile.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005| title=Fragile remnants|author=Peter Noever, ‎Angela Völker | page=57| passage=Another '''tabula''' in Paris shows the same characteristics but even more progressively stylized and is therefore dated to the Arabian period, eighth to ninth century.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006| title=HALI: The International Journal of Oriental Carpets and Textiles|author=| page=111| passage=Another '''tabula''' ( cat.89 ) recalls the naturalistic concept of the classic Graeco-Roman portrait, in widespread use up to the 4th or 5th century. }}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|historical}} An ancient [[Roman]] [[game]] similar to [[backgammon]] that was [[play]]ed on a [[board]] with 24 divisions.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974| title=Games & Puzzles - Issues 21-31 |author=| page=5| passage=Readers may like to make a '''tabula''' board and try a simple game from France using two cubic dice.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002| title=Sports and Games of the Ancients |author=Steve Craig | page=113| passage=A bronze mirror from about 200 B.C. depicts a young man and a young woman playing '''tabula'''. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011| title=Hereward|author=James Wilde | page=209| passage=Two men hunched in silence over black and white counters on a '''tabula''' board which had been carved around the edge with scenes of warriors at arms.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015| title=Ascendant's Rite|author=David Hair | page=| passage=Such was his urgency, he entered the healers' wagon without warning those inside, which was a mistake, for Lanna was playing '''tabula''' with Ramon on the rear pallet – and both were naked.}}
# {{lb|en|zoology}} One of the [[transverse]] [[plant]]s found in the [[calicle]]s of certain [[coral]]s and [[hydroid]]s.
#* {{quote-book |en |title=[[w:Encyclopædia Britannica|The Encyclopædia Britannica: a Dictionary of Arts, Sciences, and General Literature]] |edition=ninth |volume=VI |year=1877 |location=Edinburgh |publisher=Adam and Charles Black |chapter=Corals |page=383 |text=The '''tabulæ''' may be well developed, approximately horizontal, remote plates, as is usually the case in ''Zaphrentis'' and ''Amplexus'', or they may anastomoze in various ways, and become so intimately connected with one another as to give rise to a species of vesicular tissue.}}
#* {{quote-book |en |title=A Manual of Palæontology for the Use of Students with a General Introduction on the Principles of Palæontology |edition=second |volume=I |author=Henry Alleyne Nicholson |authorlink=Henry Alleyne Nicholson |location=Edinburgh and London |publisher=[[w:Blackwood (publishing house)|William Blackwood and Sons]] |year=1879 |page=183 |text=When fully developed (fig. 66), they are transverse plates, which extend completely across the visceral chamber, and divide it into a series of stories placed one above the other, the only living portion of the coral being above the last formed '''tabula'''. '''Tabulæ''' are found in various of the ''Zoantharia sclerodermata'', in some of the ''Alcyonaria'', and in a great many of the ''Rugosa''.}}
#* {{quote-journal |en |journal=[[w:Journal of the Geological Society|The Quarterly Journal of the Geological Society of London]] |volume=the fortieth |year=1884 |page=501 |text=Septa from 110 to 124, of two orders. The principal ones can be traced almost to the centre as crests on the '''tabulæ'''. The secondaries scarcely attain 4 millimetres, including the epitheca. They often bend towards the primaries, and are united by a few dissepiments (or rather the subdivided margins of the '''tabulæ'''). These latter are large, and virtually extend across the entire visceral chamber. The fossula (well seen in fig. 4) is formed by a deep inflexion of the '''tabulæ''', the septa bending round with the margin of the depression.}}
#* {{quote-book |en |title=Atlantide Report: Scientific Results of the Danish Expedition to the Coasts of Tropical West Africa, 1945-1946 |author=Danish Science Press |year=1968 |publisher=Aarhuus Stiftsbogtrykkerie |page=220 |text=Frontal with a '''tabula''', with a crescent of small, slit-like pores.}}
#* {{quote-book |en |title=Drawing & Understanding Fossils: A Theoretical and Practical Guide for Beginners, with Self-assessment |author=E. W. Nield |year=1987 |isbn=1483286525 |publisher=[[w:Pergamon Press|Pergamon Press]] |page=92 |text={{smc|Fig.}} 12.5. Tabulate, rugose and scleractinian corals. i. Portion of a colony of ''Favosites''. Note the very minute corallites closely packed together. ii. The chain coral ''Halysites'', one corallite partially cut away to expose '''tabulae'''.}}
#* {{quote-book |en |title=Bull. K. Belg. Inst. Nat. Wet |year=1972 |page=69 |text=In the axially sectioned, middle part of the corallite, '''tabulae''' are complete, leaning at approximately 50° from the corallite wall to its axis.}}
#* {{quote-book |en |title=New Zealand Oceanographic Institute Memoir |year=1989 |page=38 |text=Dactylostyles absent but, at and below coenosteal level, dactylopore tubes contain a series of incomplete '''tabulae''' (herein termed pseudotabulae), each approximately 10 μm thick and spaced 50–70 μm apart (Plate 21, c, d0. Pseudo-'''tabulae''' originate from opposing lateral and sometimes anterior edges of the dactylopore tube but never quite meet to form complete '''tabulae'''.}}
#* {{quote-book |en |title=Australian Bryozoa |volume=2: “Taxonomy of Australian Families” |editors=Patricia L. Cook, Philip E. Bock, Dennis P. Gordon, Haylee J. Weaver |year=2018 |publisher=[[w:CSIRO Publishing|CSIRO Publishing]] |isbn=1486306837 |chapter=3. Class Gymnolaemata, Order Cheilostomata |author=Patricia L. Cook |author2=Philip E. Bock |author3=Peter J. Hayward |author4=Dennis P. Gordon |section=“7. Taxonomic treatments of families of Cheilostomata”, “Australian diversity” |page=203 |text=The latter differs strictly from ''Buffonellodes'' in lacking oral spines, and in possessing an ectooecial '''tabula''' and supplementary acute frontal avicularia, and requires a new genus.}}

